{
  "title": "Maximum Depth of Binary Tree",
  "description": "Number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path.",
  "statement": "A binary tree is given as a hierarchy of records. Report how deep the tree goes.\n\nThe **depth** of a node is the number of nodes on the longest path from the root down to that node, counting the root itself. The maximum depth of the tree is the largest such value, which is equivalently the number of nodes on the longest root-to-leaf path.\n\nAn empty subtree has depth 0 and a leaf has depth 1, so a tree made of a single root has depth 1 and the answer counts **nodes**, not edges. The maximum depth of a node is one more than the deeper of its two children.",
  "inputFormat": "The input is read from standard input as exactly one line:\n\n1. Line 1: the tree in level-order (breadth-first) serialisation. Values are separated by commas with **no spaces**, and the literal `null` marks a missing child. The tree always has at least one node (up to `10^5` nodes).",
  "outputFormat": "Print a single line containing the maximum depth as a single integer.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= number of nodes <= 10^5",
    "-10^4 <= node value <= 10^4",
    "The level-order line contains no spaces around the commas",
    "Depth is measured in nodes, so a single-node tree has depth 1"
  ],
  "notes": "Watch the off-by-one: the root alone is depth 1, and a `null` child contributes 0. The test data includes chains over 1000 nodes deep, which overflows the call stack of a recursive solution in some languages - a breadth-first sweep that counts levels handles every shape safely. Remember that the serialisation is breadth-first, so the deepest node is not simply the last token.",
  "difficulty": "EASY",
  "tags": [
    "tree",
    "recursion",
    "queue"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
  "memoryLimitKb": 256000,
  "starterCode": {
    "python": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    levelOrder = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    # TODO: print the result\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n",
    "javascript": "const fs = require('fs');\nconst levelOrder = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim();\nconst tokens = levelOrder.length === 0 ? [] : levelOrder.split(',');\n// TODO: print the result\n",
    "java": "import java.io.BufferedReader;\nimport java.io.InputStreamReader;\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = br.readLine();\n        List<String> tokens = new ArrayList<>();\n        if (line != null && !line.isEmpty()) {\n            for (String part : line.split(\",\")) {\n                tokens.add(part.trim());\n            }\n        }\n        // TODO: print the result\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    string line;\n    getline(cin, line);\n    vector<string> tokens;\n    stringstream ss(line);\n    string token;\n    while (getline(ss, token, ',')) {\n        tokens.push_back(token);\n    }\n\n    // TODO: print the result\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
